Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::string line;
    std::istringstream markdownStream(markdown);

    while (std::getline(markdownStream, line)) {
        std::istringstream lineStream(line);
        std::string word;

        while (std::getline(lineStream, word)) {
            if (word.empty()) continue;
            html += "<p>" + word + "</p>";
        }
    }

    return html;
}
```

This function takes a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. It uses `std::istringstream` to read the markdown text line by line, and then splits each line into individual words. Each word is then wrapped in a `<p>` tag and appended to the `html` string.

Note that this function assumes that the markdown text is formatted in a simple way, with each line being a separate paragraph and each word being a separate item in the paragraph. If your markdown text is more complex, you may need to modify the function to handle additional cases.

